   "claims": [
    {"claim_id": "c-c-hard-soft", "text": "The letter c has two sounds: a hard /k/ (cat, cup, cot) and a soft /s/ (city, cent). The soft sound usually comes before e, i, or y.",
     "kind": "language-usage", "source_refs": ["Cambridge Dictionary"],
     "verification_ref": "字母 c 有兩個音：硬音 /k/（cat／cup／cot）、軟音 /s/（city／cent）；軟音通常出現在 e、i、y 前；採美式／KK；Cambridge Dictionary 讀音可查；待英語母語 reviewer 複核", "risk_class": "normal", "status": "provisional"},
    {"claim_id": "c-g-hard-soft", "text": "The letter g has two sounds: a hard /ɡ/ (go, gum, got) and a soft /dʒ/ (giant, gem, gym). The soft sound usually comes before e, i, or y.",
     "kind": "language-usage", "source_refs": ["Cambridge Dictionary"],
     "verification_ref": "字母 g 有兩個音：硬音 /ɡ/（go／gum／got）、軟音 /dʒ/（giant／gem／gym）；軟音通常出現在 e、i、y 前；採美式／KK；Cambridge Dictionary 讀音可查；待英語母語 reviewer 複核", "risk_class": "normal", "status": "provisional"},
    {"claim_id": "c-cg-exceptions", "text": "The soft rule for c and g is a helpful pattern, not a law. A few common words break it: 'get' and 'give' are spelled with g before e or i yet keep the hard /ɡ/. Learn these as exceptions.",
     "kind": "language-usage", "source_refs": ["Cambridge Dictionary"],
     "verification_ref": "c／g 軟音規則是很好用的規律但非絕對：get、give 雖是 g 接 e／i 卻仍發硬音 /ɡ/，當作例外記；誠實標出例外、不教成無例外規則；Cambridge Dictionary 讀音可查；待英語母語 reviewer 複核", "risk_class": "normal", "status": "provisional"},
    {"claim_id": "c-final-le", "text": "When a word ends in a consonant plus 'le' (apple, table, little, candle), that ending makes one small syllable, the sound /əl/. The 'e' does not make its own sound; the 'l' carries the weak vowel /ə/ (schwa).",
     "kind": "language-usage", "source_refs": ["Cambridge Dictionary"],
     "verification_ref": "字尾「子音＋le」（apple／table／little／candle）發一個小音節 /əl/；字母 e 不單獨發音，弱母音 /ə/（schwa）由 l 帶出；採美式／KK；Cambridge Dictionary 讀音可查；待英語母語 reviewer 複核", "risk_class": "normal", "status": "provisional"}
   ],

   "teach": [
    {"kind": "text", "kp_ids": ["eng.t_base.phonics.hard-soft-cg"],
     "body": "The letters c and g each have two sounds: a hard one and a soft one. c is hard /k/ in cat, but soft /s/ in city. g is hard /ɡ/ in go, but soft /dʒ/ in giant. A useful clue: the soft sound usually comes before the letters e, i, or y.",
     "zh_hint": "字母 c 和 g 各有兩個音：硬音和軟音。c 在 cat 裡是硬音 /k/，在 city 裡是軟音 /s/；g 在 go 裡是硬音 /ɡ/，在 giant 裡是軟音 /dʒ/。有個好用的線索：軟音通常出現在 e、i、y 這三個字母前面。"},
    {"kind": "example", "kp_ids": ["eng.t_base.phonics.hard-soft-cg"], "audio": "ph_cg_ex1",
     "body": "cat, city, go, giant.",
     "zh_hint": "成對聽硬音和軟音：cat（c＝/k/）→city（c＝/s/）；go（g＝/ɡ/）→giant（g＝/dʒ/）。"},
    {"kind": "text", "kp_ids": ["eng.t_base.phonics.hard-soft-cg"],
     "body": "The soft rule is a helpful pattern, but not a law. A few common words break it: 'get' and 'give' are spelled with g before e or i, yet they keep the hard /ɡ/ sound. Just learn these few words as exceptions.",
     "zh_hint": "軟音規則很好用，但不是絕對的。有幾個常見字是例外：get 和 give 雖然是 g 接在 e 或 i 前面，卻還是發硬音 /ɡ/。這幾個字就當作例外記起來。"},
    {"kind": "text", "kp_ids": ["eng.t_base.phonics.final-le"],
     "body": "Many words end in a consonant plus 'le', like apple, table, and little. This ending makes one small, soft syllable that sounds like /əl/. The 'e' does not make its own sound; the 'l' carries a weak vowel. So 'table' is said like 'ta-bul', and 'little' like 'lit-tul'.",
     "zh_hint": "很多字的結尾是「一個子音＋le」，像 apple、table、little。這個結尾會發成一個小小、輕輕的音節，聽起來像 /əl/。字母 e 不單獨發音，弱弱的母音由 l 帶出。所以 table 唸起來像「ta-bul」、little 像 「lit-tul」。"},
    {"kind": "example", "kp_ids": ["eng.t_base.phonics.final-le"], "audio": "ph_cg_ex2",
     "body": "apple, table, little.",
     "zh_hint": "聽字尾輕輕的 /əl/ 音節：apple、table、little。"}
   ],

